+#### Token helpers
+
+`tokenHelper` names a program that npm runs to get the credential for a registry, instead of reading a token stored in a file.
+It is pnpm's setting of the same name, so one helper serves both package managers from the same file:
+
+```ini
+//npm.corp.example.com/:tokenHelper=/usr/local/bin/corp-registry-token
+```
+
+The value is one absolute path with no arguments.
+It may contain spaces; unlike pnpm, npm does not split it into a command and arguments, and a bare name is refused so that a program in a project cannot be chosen through `PATH`.
+A command that needs arguments goes in a two-line script, and the setting names the script.
+For example, for AWS CodeArtifact, on Linux and macOS (`chmod +x` it):
+
+```sh
+#!/bin/sh
+exec aws codeartifact get-authorization-token --domain my-domain --domain-owner 111122223333 --query authorizationToken --output text
+```
+
+and on Windows, where a `.cmd` file is run through `cmd.exe` (a path containing `"`, `%` or `!` is refused, and so is a directory path longer than 258 characters) and a `.ps1` or `.js` file needs a `.cmd` wrapper that starts it:
+
+```bat
+@echo off
+aws codeartifact get-authorization-token --domain my-domain --domain-owner 111122223333 --query authorizationToken --output text
+```
+
+The program prints the credential on one line.
+A raw token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, and output that starts with an auth scheme and a space, such as `Basic …`, is used as the header value as it is.
+Spaces around the output and a byte order mark are removed.
+A registry that has both a `tokenHelper` and a stored `_authToken` uses the helper, and npm warns once, naming the credential it ignores.
+While an OIDC trusted-publishing token is in use for a registry, it is sent instead of the helper's credential.
+`npm login` refuses for a registry with a helper, and `npm logout` says that the setting has to be removed.
+
+The helper is run the first time a request to that registry needs a credential, once per npm process, and its outcome is kept for the rest of the process: a failure is not retried either.
+Since it is not told which request it serves and cannot say how long its token is good for, a program that is slow or needs a login should keep what it fetched, in a cache of its own, so that starting it is cheap.
+
+The helper does not see the project.
+It starts in its own directory, with nothing on its standard input, and has 60 seconds and 1 MiB of output; npm ends it with SIGTERM and, 2 seconds later, SIGKILL (on Windows, the whole process tree), so a helper script should `exec` the command that does the work. Once the helper has exited npm reads its output for at most 250 ms more, so a background process it left running does not keep npm waiting.
+Its environment is npm's own without what npm set from a project's configuration: the `npm_config_*` variables, and `NODE_OPTIONS`, `EDITOR`, `NODE_ENV` and `COLOR` where npm set them from its configuration, which get the values the user's environment had.
+When npm runs inside `npm run`, the `node_modules/.bin` directories and `node-gyp-bin` it added in front of `PATH` are removed, so the helper's tools are found where the user installed them, and a tool that only a project installs is called by its absolute path.
+An `npm` that the helper itself runs finds the variable `NPM_TOKEN_HELPER` and starts no helper.
+Lines the helper writes to standard error are shown as warnings when it fails and at `--loglevel verbose` when it works, with the token it printed replaced by `***`.
+
+If a request fails with `ETOKENHELPER`, run it again with `--loglevel verbose` to see the helper's output.
+The message names the cause: a value that is not an absolute path or has arguments, a helper that is not found or cannot be started (for a script, its `#!` interpreter is named), a non-zero exit status or a signal, an empty token, output that is more than one line or not printable ASCII (the message does not quote it), more than 1 MiB of output, or more than 60 seconds.
+Exit status 127 means that a tool the script calls was not found: give its absolute path.
+npm does not fall back to another credential for that registry.
+
+Because npm executes the program, `tokenHelper` is honored only from the per-user and global config files, and only when a project `.npmrc` has not chosen them: the settings `userconfig`, `globalconfig` and `prefix` (compared after `${VAR}` expansion) must not be set in a project `.npmrc`, or the helper in those files is ignored with a warning.
+It is also ignored with a warning in a project `.npmrc`, in `publishConfig`, in `npm_config_*` environment variables and on the command line.
+Unlike pnpm, npm does not accept an unscoped `tokenHelper` for the default registry, since a project can change which registry is the default, nor a helper per package scope (`//host/:@scope:tokenHelper`) or a key without the slash before the colon (`//host:tokenHelper`); each is ignored with a warning that names the line to write instead.
+`npm config set` refuses the unscoped form and project config.
+
+The credential is sent as a stored `_authToken` for the same registry is: with the same proxy and certificate settings, the same rules for `http:` registries and the same redirects.
+
 ### Unsupported Custom Configuration Keys
 
 Starting in npm v11.2.0, npm warns when unknown configuration keys are defined in `.npmrc`. In a future major version of npm, these unknown keys may no longer be accepted.
